<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōnen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1133–<a href="/years/1212">1212</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Honen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hōnen was a Japanese Buddhist monk who founded the Jōdo-shū, the Pure Land school, in 1175. He taught that salvation came through calling on the name of the Buddha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> (the nembutsu) rather than through difficult practices, opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to ordinary people. Opposed by the older schools, he was exiled in 1207, and his disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honen.jpg" alt="Hōnen" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara, Takanobu (1142-1205) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takanobu-no-miei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
